AB  - The Indo-Pacific hump-backed dolphin (Sousa chinensis Osbeck, 1765) is found through western Pacific and Indian oceans, from southern China and northern Australia in the east to South A in the west. Throughout most of its range it has not been well studied, and in southern China very l known of its biology. The goal of the present study was to provide scientific information needed for term conservation and management of the population that occurs in Hong Kong waters. From S 1995 to November 1998, 38,105 km of systematic line transect surveys were conducted throughou waters of the Hong Kong Special Administrative Region (SAR) of the People's Republic of China (P adjacent waters to provide data on distribution and abundance. Photo-identification of individual allowed for examination of movement patterns, home ranges, and social organization. Collection of st dolphin carcasses and detailed necropsies provided information on causes of death as well as samples f history studies, such as feeding habits, growth and reproduction, ecotoxicology, and stock structure. The dolphin population appears to be centered around the Pearl River Estuary, and Hong Kong represent the eastern portion of the range, which extends far into mainland Chinese waters (Lingdin and covers at least 1,800 km2. Within Hong Kong, dolphins only occur in western waters around Island. The area north of Lantau Island is heavily used throughout the year and represents by far important habitat in Hong Kong. Line transect analyses indicate that between 88 (spring) and 145 dolphins occur in Hong Kong. Based on 27,600 photographs taken, 213 individual dolphins were id The total size of the Pearl River breeding population is unknown, but is estimated to consist of at lea dolphins, based on line transect analysis. An apparent decline in the number of dolphins in the North area over the period from 1996-1998 was not statistically significant. Individual dolphins have ov home ranges of about 30-400 km2 in different sections of the population's overall range. Groups of d in Hong Kong number up to 23 animals, with an average group size of 3.8 + s.d. 3.63 animals. Th significant seasonal variation in group size, but groups feeding behind pair trawl fishing vessels (mea + s.d. 5.37) are significantly larger than other groups, and groups in Lingding Bay, in Chinese wa of Hong Kong (mean = 8.3 + s.d. 7.84) are significantly larger than those in Hong Kong. Groups fluid and change composition frequently, with association indices ranging from 0-0.333. Behavioral p are similar to those of other coastal dolphins, but Hong Kong hump-backed dolphins only rarely waves. Following pair trawlers represents an important feeding strategy for some individuals. Constr work on an airport fuel facility in the dolphins' main habitat appears to have caused some disturb dicated by increased swimming speeds) and possibly temporary evacuation of the surrounding area.
