AB  - The diet of bottlenose dolphins in southeast Asia is poorly known. Two forms of bottlenose dolphins occur oﬀ Hong Kong; an inshore form (aduncustype) and an oﬀshore form (truncatus-type), which diﬀer in total length and other morphological features. We examined the stomach contents of four oﬀshore bottlenose dolphins stranded between 1994 and 1997. Nine species of ﬁsh (of nine families) and three species of cephalopods (of three families) were identiﬁed as prey. These species are found in neritic and pelagic habitats, and represent both surfacedwellers as well as demersal inhabitants. Whereas some prey species may be found close to shore, others extend their distribution into deeper waters, suggesting that the dolphins may have also fed in inshore waters in the process of stranding. Although sample sizes are small, little competition with other cetacean species found around Hong Kong (Indo–Paciﬁc humpback dolphins and ﬁnless porpoises) is suspected, as few prey species were shared by these dolphins.
