TI  - The identification of critically endangered yangtze finless porpoise high-density areas for priority protection
T2  - Mammalian Biology
LA  - English
KW  - /unread
AB  - The Nanjing section of the Yangtze River has served as a constant habitat for the endangered Yangtze finless porpoise (Neophocaena asiaeorientalis asiaeorientalis, YFP). A provincial nature reserve was established in Nanjing area in 2014; however, the abundance and distribution pattern of YFP have not been reported in this century. From April 2017 to December 2018, 93 vessel-based surveys were conducted using the line-transect method, which recorded 356 sightings of 1243 cumulative animals along the 10,130.28 km survey track effort. The encounter rate was 0.035 sightings/km and 0.12 porpoises/ km, and the median group size was 3 ranging from 1 to 16 individuals. Also, the Distance software generated a population density of 0.56 individuals/km2 [95% confidence interval: 0.46–0.67], representing a higher density of YFPs compared to other populations in the Yangtze River so far. The annual and seasonal variations in YFP density were found to be as follows: the density in 2017 (0.56 individuals/km2) was higher than that in 2018 (0.45 individuals/km2), and the density during the winter/dry season (1.20 individuals/km2) was higher than during other seasons (0.58 individuals/km2 during spring transition, 0.59 individuals/km2 during summer/wet season, and 0.46 individuals/km2 during the autumn transition season). YFPs occurred in the Nanjing section throughout the year, indicating a resident population, and aggregated at three hotspots. The high-density and year-round occurrence of YFPs in Nanjing suggested the urgent need to prioritize their in-situ protection. At present, certain effective measures, such as banning fishing, have been implemented; however, YFPs still get disturbed due to other anthropogenic activities. It is recommended to maintain the current natural habitat, recover the natural coastline, limit the shipping speed, and adjust core zones of the reserve to protect the YFPs.
