TI  - Long-term changes in habitat use of indo-pacific humpback dolphins (sousa chinensis) under the stress of human activities in the eastern pearl river estuary, China
T2  - Marine Pollution Bulletin
LA  - English
KW  - /unread
AB  - The Pearl River Estuary (PRE) on China’s South China Sea coast supports the world’s largest Indo-Pacific humpback dolphin population but is increasingly threatened by development. Using line-transect sighting data (1997–2021) and historical Landsat imagery, we quantified long-term habitat change in the eastern PRE. About 351.4 km2 of water was lost between 1973 and 2020. Dolphins persistently favored natural shorelines; nevertheless, total range and 50 % core-use areas peaked in 2005–2006 and contracted sharply by 2020–2021, fragmenting into isolated patches. A new core area appeared near the Macau–Hong Kong ferry routes during the COVID-19 traffic lull, illustrating rapid behavioural plasticity. The proportion of core habitat within the Guangdong Dolphin Reserve fell from 79.9 % (1997) to 49.4 % (2016) before partial recovery in 2020–2021. We recommend realigning reserve boundaries and imposing stricter controls on marine construction to mitigate ongoing habitat loss. Proactive, adaptive management is essential for sustaining this vulnerable population.
