AB  - Quantitative evaluating beach cleanness and litter hazardous levels is the first step for effective action planning to control beach litter pollution. In the present study, density, compositions and spatial distribution patterns of beach litter in the Sanniang Bay, Qinzhou City, Guangxi were investigated. Clean-coast index (CCI) and Hazardous anthropogenic beach litter index (HABLI) were used to evaluate beach cleanness and litter hazardous level. The density of beach litter number and weight in Sanniang Bay were (531100±207200) items/km2 and (3553.01±2698.06) kg/km2. Litter densities in cape areas and shipping areas were higher than those in straight beaches and tourism areas. Plastic litter accounted for 71.02%, while 37.5% beach litter came from coastal anthropogenic activities. Litter size was dominated by medium-sized litter (61.56%). CCIs and HABLIs indicated the Sanniang Bay was "dirty" (CCI = 10.40), but "moderately safe" (HABLI = 0.23).
