TI  - In vitro immunotoxicity and risk assessments of banned polychlorinated biphenyls exposure in indo-pacific humpback dolphins in the pearl river estuary from the northern south China sea
T2  - Environmental Research
LA  - English
KW  - /unread
AB  - Exposure to banned polychlorinated biphenyls (PCBs) presents significant concerns for cetacean health due to their detrimental effects. Our prior research documented elevated toxic equivalency levels of PCBs, predominantly PCB126 (up to 95.5 %), in fetal Indo-Pacific humpback dolphins (Sousa chinensis) from the Pearl River Estuary (PRE) between 2003 and 2020. Critical knowledge gaps persist, however, regarding the cytotoxic effects and mechanisms of PCB126 exposure in these dolphins. To address this, we evaluated the in vitro immunotoxicity and associated risks of PCB126 using PRE humpback dolphin samples (n = 128, representing stranded animals). High PCB126 concentrations induced cytotoxicity in skin fibroblasts and significantly upregulated the expression of inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, IL-1α, TNFα). Additionally, we observed NF-κB nuclear translocation, indicating activation mediated via the aryl hydrocarbon receptor pathway. PCB126 exposure further elevated ICAM-1 and IL-8 expression, suggesting a potential contribution to chronic localized inflammation. The correlation between IL-6 expression and PCB126 concentrations implies IL-6 may serve as a sensitive biomarker for immune stress in humpback dolphins. Exposure-activity ratio (EAR) assessments indicate PRE humpback dolphin populations face a risk of chronic inflammation from PCB126. This study advances understanding of the cellular health impacts of individual PCB congeners and underscores the persistent threat of organic pollutants to cetacean conservation.
